      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-15589",
      ":question/problem": "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{\u2218}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{\u2218}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{\u2218}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{\u2218}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*"
